msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:37
msgid ""
"The template parameters of the selected GEMM kernel are classified into "
"four groups. These template parameter groups should be defined properly "
"before running the instance."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:42
msgid "Matrix data precision"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:44
msgid ""
"A, B, D, and E are defined as half-precision floating-point datatypes. "
"The multiply-add results of matrix A and B are added with a pre-existing "
"matrix D (half-precision), and the final GEMM results are also half-"
"precision floating-points."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:55
msgid ""
"`ADataType` and `BDataType` denote the data precision of the A and B "
"input matrices. `AccDataType` determines the data precision used for "
"representing the multiply-add results of A and B elements. These results "
"are stored in a `CShuffle` module in local data share (LDS), a low-"
"latency and high-bandwidth explicitly-addressed memory used for "
"synchronization within a workgroup LDS for later use."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:57
msgid "`CShuffleDataType` denotes the data precision of `CShuffle` in LDS."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:59
msgid ""
"`DDataType` denotes the data precision of the pre-existing D matrix "
"stored in GPU global memory, while `EDatatype` denotes the data precision"
" of the final output. The CK kernel supports a fusion strategy so that "
"`CShuffle` can be added with a single pre-existing matrix in the same GPU"
" kernel for better performance."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:63
msgid "Matrix data layout"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:72
msgid ""
"Following the convention of various linear algebra libraries, CK assumes "
"that the input matrix A is an M x K matrix, meaning the matrix has M rows"
" and K columns. Similarly, matrix B is assumed to be K x N, meaning it "
"has K rows and N columns. In computing, row-major order and column-major "
"order are commonly used ways to store matrices in linear storage. After "
"understanding the matrix storage pattern, the underlying optimized memory"
" access manner can be applied to achieve better performance depending on "
"the storage ordering of these matrices."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:76
msgid "Matrix element operation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:84
msgid ""
"CK supports the pre-processing of the matrix before calculating GEMM, "
"that is, `C = AElementOp(A) * BElementOp(B)`. It similarly supports the "
"post-processing of GEMM results the same way, that is, `E = "
"CDEElementOp(C, D)`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:86
msgid ""
"`AElementOp` and `BElementOp` determine the operation applied to matrix A"
" and B separately before GEMM, which is achieved by binding the operation"
" with a C++ struct function."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:88
msgid ""
"The above `PassThrough` denotes no operations are performed on the target"
" matrix. `CDEELementOp` determines the operations applied to `CShuffle` "
"output and matrix D. The following binding struct `AddRelu` shows an "
"example of adding the `CShuffle` output and matrix D, and ReLU (Rectified"
" Linear Unit) operations to the addition result. It then passes the "
"results to matrix E."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:103
msgid "Tunable parameters"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:105
msgid ""
"The CK instance includes a series of tunable template parameters to "
"control the parallel granularity of the workload to achieve load "
"balancing on different hardware platforms."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:107
msgid ""
"These parameters include Block Size, M/N/K Per Block, M/N per XDL, AK1, "
"BK1, etc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:109
msgid "Block Size determines the number of threads in the thread block."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:110
msgid ""
"M/N/K Per Block determines the size of tile that each thread block is "
"responsible for calculating."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:111
msgid ""
"M/N Per XDL refers to M/N size for Instinct GPU Matrix Fused Multiply Add"
" (MFMA) instructions operating on a per-wavefront basis."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:112
msgid ""
"A/B K1 is related to the data type. It can be any value ranging from 1 to"
" K Per Block. To achieve the optimal load/store performance, 128bit per "
"load is suggested. In addition, the A/B loading parameters must be "
"changed accordingly to match the A/B K1 value; otherwise, it will result "
"in compilation errors."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:114
msgid ""
"Conditions for achieving computational load balancing on different "
"hardware platforms can vary."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:116
msgid "Instantiating and running the templated kernel"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:118
msgid ""
"After determining the template parameters, we instantiate the kernel with"
" actual arguments. Do one of the following:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:120
msgid ""
"Use `GetDeviceBuffer` from CK’s custom struct `DeviceMem` to pass the "
"element values of the matrices that need to be calculated."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:121
msgid ""
"Allocate device buffer via `hipMalloc`. Ensure the device buffer size can"
" fit the matrix size."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:122
msgid ""
"Pass matrix elements through the `data_ptr` method in the `Tensor` object"
" if the matrix to be calculated is of `Tensor` type."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:124
msgid ""
"The row and column, and stride information of input matrices are also "
"passed to the instance. For batched GEMM, you must pass in additional "
"batch count and batch stride values. The extra operations for pre and "
"post-processing are also passed with an actual argument; for example, α "
"and β for GEMM scaling operations. Afterward, the instantiated kernel is "
"launched by the invoker, as illustrated in Figure 3."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:131
msgid ""
"Templated kernel launching consists of kernel instantiation, making "
"arguments by passing in actual application parameters, creating an "
"invoker, and running the instance through the invoker."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:134
msgid "Developing fused INT8 kernels for SmoothQuant models"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:136
msgid ""
"[SmoothQuant](https://github.com/mit-han-lab/smoothquant) (SQ) is a "
"quantization algorithm that enables an INT8 quantization of both weights "
"and activations for all the matrix multiplications in LLM. The required "
"GPU kernel functionalities used to accelerate the inference of SQ models "
"on Instinct GPUs are shown in the following table."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:138
msgid "Functionalities used to implement SmoothQuant model inference."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:149
msgid "Operation flow analysis"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:151
msgid ""
"The following section discusses the analysis of the operation flow of "
"`Linear_ReLU_ABDE_I8`. The rest of the wrappers in Table 1 can be "
"analyzed similarly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:153
msgid ""
"The first operation in the process is to perform the multiplication of "
"input matrices A and B. The resulting matrix C is then scaled with α to "
"obtain T1. At the same time, the process performs a scaling operation on "
"D elements to obtain T2. Afterward, the process performs matrix addition "
"between T1 and T2, element activation calculation using ReLU, and element"
" rounding sequentially. The operations to generate E1, E2, and E are "
"encapsulated and completed by a user-defined template function in CK "
"(given in the next sub-section). This template function is integrated "
"into the fundamental instance directly during the compilation phase so "
"that all these steps can be fused in a single GPU kernel."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:160
msgid "Operation flow."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:163
msgid ""
"The CK library contains many fundamental instances that implement "
"different functions. Familiarize yourself with the names of various CK "
"instances and determine whether they meet the target functional "
"requirements."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:165
msgid ""
"Second, consider whether the format of input data meets your actual "
"calculation needs. For SQ models, the 8-bit integer data format (INT8) is"
" applied for matrix calculations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:167
msgid ""
"Third, consider the platform for implementing CK instances. The instances"
" suffixed with `xdl` only run on AMD Instinct GPUs after being compiled "
"and cannot run on Radeon-Series GPUs. This is due to the underlying "
"device-specific instruction sets for implementing these basic instances."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:169
msgid ""
"Here, we use "
"[DeviceBatchedGemmMultiD_Xdl](https://github.com/ROCm/composable_kernel/tree/develop/example/24_batched_gemm)"
" as the fundamental instance to implement the functionalities in the "
"previous table."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:176
msgid "Use the ‘DeviceBatchedGemmMultiD_Xdl’ instance as a root."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:179
msgid ""
"The `DeviceBatchedGemmMultiD_Xdl` instance realizes the batched GEMM "
"`BMM_ABE_I8` and `BMM_AB_I8_E_F32` kernels directly by using the proper "
"input and output data precision types."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:181
msgid ""
"Based on the two batched GEMM kernels, GEMM kernel `Linear_ABDE_I8` and "
"`Linear_AB_I8_DE_F32` can be implemented by expanding their input 2-D "
"tensors to 3-D tensors. Then, the 3-D output tensors produced by the root"
" instance are squeezed back to 2-D output tensors before returning back."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:183
msgid ""
"For example, unsqueeze A (M, K) to A (1, M, K) before assigning it into "
"the root instance and squeeze E (1, M, N) to (M, N) after the "
"calculations of the root instance return back. `Linear_ReLU_ABDE_I8` is "
"implemented by adding a ReLU operation on the result output of "
"`Linear_ABDE_I8`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:185
msgid "Developing the complete function"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:187
msgid ""
"The inference of SQ quantized models relies on using PyTorch and "
"Transformer libraries, and a tensor type is used to represent matrices "
"and vectors in `torch`, the C++ data types in CK need to be replaced with"
" the `torch::tensor` type. The data types of the input and output "
"matrices should be a `tensor` type."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:189
msgid ""
"In GEMM, the A and B inputs are two-dimensional matrices, and the "
"required input matrices of the selected fundamental CK instance are "
"three-dimensional matrices. Therefore, we must convert the input 2-D "
"tensors to 3-D tensors, by using `tensor`'s `unsqueeze()` method before "
"passing these matrices to the instance. For batched GEMM in the preceding"
" table, ignore this step."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:208
msgid ""
"As shown in the following code block, we obtain M, N, and K values using "
"input tensor size values. This stride size information is used to reshape"
" the input vector D and allocate the storage space of tensor E. Stride "
"reflects the exact size of continuous elements in memory, which are "
"passed as important parameters to the fundamental instance for GPU kernel"
" use."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:239
msgid ""
"In the following code block, `ADataType`, `BDataType` and `D0DataType` "
"are used to denote the data precision of the input tensors A, B and D, "
"respectively. `EDataType` is used to denote the data precision of output "
"tensor E. These parameters are specified to `I8` data format (8-bit "
"integer data format) to meet the kernel's design requirements."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:241
msgid ""
"`AccDataType` determines the data precision used to represent the "
"multiply-add results of A and B elements. Generally, a larger range data "
"type is applied to store the multiply-add results of A and B to avoid "
"result overflow; `I32` is applied in this case. The `CShuffleDataType "
"I32` data type indicates that the multiply-add results continue to be "
"stored in LDS as an `I32` data format. All of this is implemented through"
" the following code block."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:254
msgid ""
"Following the convention of various linear algebra libraries, row-major "
"and column-major orders are used to denote the ways of storing matrices "
"in linear storage. The advantage of specifying matrix B as column major "
"is that all the relevant matrix elements are stored continuously in GPU "
"global memory when a row in A is multiplied by a column in B, which can "
"help GPU achieve data consistency access to improve access performance."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:265
msgid ""
"In CK, `PassThrough` is a struct denoting if an operation is applied to "
"the tensor it binds to. To fuse the operations between E1, E2, and E "
"introduced in section [Operation flow analysis](#operation-flow-"
"analysis), we define a custom C++ struct, `ScaleScaleAddRelu`, and bind "
"it to `CDEELementOp`. It determines the operations that will be applied "
"to `CShuffle` (A×B results), tensor D, α, and β."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/optimizing-with-composable-kernel.md:276
msgid ""
"In the binding struct, `operator()` performs an addition operation "
"between `CShuffle` and matrix D, a ReLU operation on the addition "
"results, and a rounding operation on the output elements. It then returns"
" the results to E."
msgstr ""

