#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:484
msgid ""
"This section discusses profiling and debugging tools and some of their "
"common usage patterns with ROCm applications."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:489
msgid "PyTorch Profiler"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:491
msgid ""
"`PyTorch Profiler <https://pytorch.org/docs/stable/profiler.html>`_ can "
"be invoked inside Python scripts, letting you collect CPU and GPU "
"performance metrics while the script is running. See the `PyTorch "
"Profiler tutorial "
"<https://pytorch.org/tutorials/recipes/recipes/profiler_recipe.html>`_ "
"for more information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:495
msgid ""
"You can then visualize and view these metrics using an open-source "
"profile visualization tool like `Perfetto UI <https://ui.perfetto.dev>`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:498
msgid "Use the following snippet to invoke PyTorch Profiler in your code."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:513
msgid ""
"Profile results in ``resnet18_profile.json`` can be viewed by the "
"Perfetto visualization tool. Go to `<https://ui.perfetto.dev>`__ and "
"import the file. In your Perfetto visualization, you'll see that the "
"upper section shows transactions denoting the CPU activities that launch "
"GPU kernels while the lower section shows the actual GPU activities where"
" it processes the ``resnet18`` inferences layer by layer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:521
msgid "Perfetto trace visualization example."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:524
msgid "ROCm profiling tools"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:526
msgid ""
"Heterogenous systems, where programs run on both CPUs and GPUs, introduce"
" additional complexities. Understanding the critical path and kernel "
"execution is all the more important. So, performance tuning is a "
"necessary component in the benchmarking process."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:530
msgid ""
"With AMD's profiling tools, developers are able to gain important insight"
" into how efficiently their application is using hardware resources and "
"effectively diagnose potential bottlenecks contributing to poor "
"performance. Developers working with AMD Instinct GPUs have multiple "
"tools depending on their specific profiling needs; these include:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:543
msgid "ROCProfiler"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:545
msgid ""
":doc:`ROCProfiler <rocprofiler:index>` is primarily a low-level API for "
"accessing and extracting GPU hardware performance metrics, commonly "
"called *performance counters*. These counters quantify the performance of"
" the underlying architecture showcasing which pieces of the computational"
" pipeline and memory hierarchy are being utilized."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:549
msgid ""
"Your ROCm installation contains a script or executable command called "
"``rocprof`` which provides the ability to list all available hardware "
"counters for your specific GPU, and run applications while collecting "
"counters during their execution."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:553
msgid ""
"This ``rocprof`` utility also depends on the :doc:`ROCTracer and ROC-TX "
"libraries <roctracer:index>`, giving it the ability to collect timeline "
"traces of the GPU software stack as well as user-annotated code regions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:558
msgid ""
"``rocprof`` is a CLI-only utility where inputs and outputs take the form "
"of text and CSV files. These formats provide a raw view of the data and "
"puts the onus on the user to parse and analyze. ``rocprof`` gives the "
"user full access and control of raw performance profiling data, but "
"requires extra effort to analyze the collected data."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:566
msgid "ROCm Compute Profiler"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:568
msgid ""
":doc:`ROCm Compute Profiler <rocprofiler-compute:index>` is a system "
"performance profiler for high-performance computing (HPC) and machine "
"learning (ML) workloads using Instinct GPUs. Under the hood, ROCm Compute"
" Profiler uses :ref:`ROCProfiler <mi300x-rocprof>` to collect hardware "
"performance counters. The ROCm Compute Profiler tool performs system "
"profiling based on all approved hardware counters for Instinct GPU "
"architectures. It provides high level performance analysis features "
"including System Speed-of-Light, IP block Speed-of-Light, Memory Chart "
"Analysis, Roofline Analysis, Baseline Comparisons, and more."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:575
msgid ""
"ROCm Compute Profiler takes the guesswork out of profiling by removing "
"the need to provide text input files with lists of counters to collect "
"and analyze raw CSV output files as is the case with ROCProfiler. "
"Instead, ROCm Compute Profiler automates the collection of all available "
"hardware counters in one command and provides graphical interfaces to "
"help users understand and analyze bottlenecks and stressors for their "
"computational workloads on AMD Instinct GPUs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:582
msgid ""
"ROCm Compute Profiler collects hardware counters in multiple passes, and "
"will therefore re-run the application during each pass to collect "
"different sets of metrics."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:588
msgid "ROCm Compute Profiler memory chart analysis panel."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:590
msgid ""
"In brief, ROCm Compute Profiler provides details about hardware activity "
"for a particular GPU kernel. It also supports both a web-based GUI or "
"command-line analyzer, depending on your preference."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:596
msgid "ROCm Systems Profiler"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:598
msgid ""
":doc:`ROCm Systems Profiler <rocprofiler-systems:index>` is a "
"comprehensive profiling and tracing tool for parallel applications, "
"including HPC and ML packages, written in C, C++, Fortran, HIP, OpenCL, "
"and Python which execute on the CPU or CPU and GPU. It is capable of "
"gathering the performance information of functions through any "
"combination of binary instrumentation, call-stack sampling, user-defined "
"regions, and Python interpreter hooks."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:603
msgid ""
"ROCm Systems Profiler supports interactive visualization of comprehensive"
" traces in the web browser in addition to high-level summary profiles "
"with ``mean/min/max/stddev`` statistics. Beyond runtime information, ROCm"
" Systems Profiler supports the collection of system-level metrics such as"
" CPU frequency, GPU temperature, and GPU utilization. Process and thread "
"level metrics such as memory usage, page faults, context switches, and "
"numerous other hardware counters are also included."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:611
msgid ""
"When analyzing the performance of an application, it is best not to "
"assume you know where the performance bottlenecks are and why they are "
"happening. ROCm Systems Profiler is the ideal tool for characterizing "
"where optimization would have the greatest impact on the end-to-end "
"execution of the application and to discover what else is happening on "
"the system during a performance bottleneck."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:619
msgid "ROCm Systems Profiler timeline trace example."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:622
msgid "vLLM performance optimization"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:624
msgid ""
"vLLM is a high-throughput and memory efficient inference and serving "
"engine for large language models that has gained traction in the AI "
"community for its performance and ease of use. See :doc:`vllm-"
"optimization`, where you'll learn how to:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:629
msgid "Enable AITER (AI Tensor Engine for ROCm) to speed up on LLM models."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:642
msgid ""
"`TunableOp "
"<https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/blob/main/aten/src/ATen/cuda/tunable/README.md>`_"
" is a feature used to obtain the optimal GPU kernel for a key PyTorch "
"operations. At the moment, TunableOp supports the tuning of dense matrix "
"multiplies (GEMM, batched GEMM, GEMM and bias, and scaled GEMM). This "
"feature is useful for squeezing out the last bit of performance. In "
"short, it will try up to thousands of matrix multiply algorithms that are"
" available in rocBLAS and hipBLASLt. A caveat is that as the math "
"libraries improve over time, there is a less benefit to using TunableOp, "
"and there is also no guarantee that the workload being tuned will be able"
" to outperform the default GEMM algorithm in hipBLASLt."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:650
msgid ""
"Some additional references for PyTorch TunableOp include `ROCm blog "
"<https://rocm.blogs.amd.com/artificial-intelligence/pytorch-"
"tunableop/README.html>`__, TunableOp `README "
"<https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/blob/main/aten/src/ATen/cuda/tunable/README.md>`__,"
" and `llm tuning <https://rocm.docs.amd.com/en/latest/how-to/llm-fine-"
"tuning-optimization/model-acceleration-libraries.html#fine-tuning-llms-"
"pytorch-tunableop>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:654
msgid ""
"The three most important environment variables for controlling TunableOp "
"are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:656
msgid "``PYTORCH_TUNABLEOP_ENABLED``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:657
msgid ""
"The main on/off switch for all TunableOp implementations. Default is "
"``0`` (disabled). Set to ``1`` to enable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:659
msgid "``PYTORCH_TUNABLEOP_TUNING``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:660
msgid ""
"When enabled, if a tuned entry isn't found, runs the tuning step and "
"records the entry. Default is ``1`` (enabled). Set to ``0`` to disable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:662
msgid "``PYTORCH_TUNABLEOP_VERBOSE``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:663
msgid ""
"Enables verbose output for debugging purposes -- it can be useful to see "
"if TunableOp is being used at all. Default is ``0`` (disabled). Set to "
"``1`` to enable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:665
msgid ""
"For the complete list of environment variables, see the TunableOp `README"
" "
"<https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/blob/main/aten/src/ATen/cuda/tunable/README.md>`__."
" There are also Python APIs to set some of these environment variables, "
"but the preferred way to set the TunableOp tuning parameters is to use "
"the environment variables."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:671
msgid "Workflow"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:673
msgid ""
"Use these environment variables to enable TunableOp for any applications "
"or libraries that use PyTorch (2.3 or later)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:675
msgid "The first step is the tuning pass:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:677
msgid "Enable TunableOp and tuning. Optionally enable verbose mode:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:683
msgid ""
"This pass can be very slow. The output will be the "
"``tunableop_results.csv`` file containing a list of GEMMs encountered and"
" the optimal GPU kernel that was identified."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:688
msgid ""
"Multi-GPU tuning is supported, producing a separate tunableop_results.csv"
" file for each GPU. The tuning algorithm executes independently on each "
"GPU, with each tuning process sandboxed to its respective GPU. There is "
"no inter-GPU communication during tuning."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:692
msgid ""
"For data-parallel algorithms, where GEMM configurations across GPUs are "
"typically identical, this approach can result in redundant work. In such "
"cases, running the workload on a single GPU might suffice. However, for "
"algorithms involving multiple levels of parallelism (as in data "
"parallelism combined with ML model parallelism), different GPUs might "
"require distinct GEMM parameters. In these scenarios, a multi-GPU "
"configuration is recommended."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:698
msgid ""
"In the second step, we re-run the workload with optimal configuration "
"using the ``tunableop_results.csv`` file obtained in step 1."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:700
msgid "Enable TunableOp, disable tuning, and measure:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:706
msgid ""
"Compare the wall-clock time from this second step to your reference wall-"
"clock time with TunableOp completely disabled "
"(``PYTORCH_TUNABLEOP_ENABLED=0``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:709
msgid "Offline tuning"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:711
msgid ""
"A new feature of TunableOp, offline tuning, is available in upstream "
"PyTorch and supported in PyTorch 2.6 or later."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:713
msgid ""
"Traditionally, tuning is performed in-place during workload execution. "
"While convenient for one-off tuning, this approach can become cumbersome "
"if frequent re-tuning is required -- such as when a new version of a math"
" library is released. In these cases, re-running the workload and "
"performing tuning repeatedly can be inefficient."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:718
msgid ""
"Offline tuning addresses this challenge by decoupling the tuning process "
"from workload execution. It enables the collection of GEMMs from a "
"workload during a collection pass, followed by tuning these GEMMs in a "
"separate tuning pass, without re-running the original workload. This "
"approach significantly reduces compute resource requirements, "
"particularly for time-intensive workloads."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:723
msgid ""
"For workflow instructions, refer to the `Offline Tuning documentation "
"<https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/blob/main/aten/src/ATen/cuda/tunable/README.md"
"#offline-tuning>`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:728
msgid "torch.compile (TorchInductor)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:730
msgid ""
"``torch.compile`` uses TorchInductor, the default PyTorch compiler "
"backend, to lower captured graphs into optimized code for CPUs and "
"accelerators. On AMD GPUs, TorchInductor uses the Triton compiler for "
"many generated kernels and also relies on ROCm libraries such as "
":doc:`MIOpen <miopen:index>`, :doc:`rocBLAS <rocblas:index>`, and "
":doc:`hipBLASLt <hipblaslt:index>` for library-backed operations. For an "
"overview of TorchInductor, see the `PyTorch compiler overview "
"<https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/user_guide/torch_compiler/torch.compiler.html>`_."
" For the ``torch.compile`` API and the available compile modes such as "
"``default``, ``reduce-overhead``, ``max-autotune``, and ``max-autotune-"
"no-cudagraphs``, see the `torch.compile documentation "
"<https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/generated/torch.compile.html>`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:745
msgid ""
"Triton is not used if regular :doc:`MIOpen <miopen:index>` or a library "
"kernel from :doc:`rocBLAS <rocblas:index>` and/or :doc:`hipBLASLt "
"<hipblaslt:index>` performs faster for a specific operation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:750
msgid "Inductor tuning knobs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:752
msgid ""
"The starting point for tuning with Inductor is to use "
"``torch.compile(mode=\"max-autotune\")``. When ``max-autotune`` is "
"enabled, all Triton kernels generated by Inductor are benchmarked and "
"optimized at compile time. These kernels include ``gemm``, ``conv``, "
"``flex attention``, ``pointwise``, ``reduction``, ``persistent "
"reduction``, and ``foreach``. A predefined list of Triton configurations "
"is benchmarked, and the fastest one is selected for each shape. On GPU, "
"``max-autotune`` also enables HIP Graphs by default. HIP Graphs can be "
"especially beneficial when Triton kernels are short in duration and "
"launch overhead is a significant fraction of runtime. Use ``mode=\"max-"
"autotune-no-cudagraphs\"`` to enable the same autotuning without HIP "
"Graphs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:765
msgid ""
"Configuration considered for ``gemm`` and ``conv`` can be found in "
"PyTorch source code:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:767
msgid ""
"`conv configurations for \"max-autotune\" "
"<https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/blob/a1d02b423c6b4ccacd25ebe86de43f650463bbc6/torch/_inductor/kernel/conv.py#L51>`_"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:769
msgid ""
"`matmul configurations for \"max-autotune\" "
"<https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/blob/a1d02b423c6b4ccacd25ebe86de43f650463bbc6/torch/_inductor/kernel/mm_common.py#L118>`_"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:771
msgid ""
"This tuning will select the best Triton ``gemm`` configurations according"
" to tile-size ``(BLOCK_M, BLOCK_N, BLOCK_K), num_stages, num_warps`` and "
"``mfma`` instruction size ( ``matrix_instr_nonkdim`` ) (see \"Triton "
"kernel optimization\" section for more details)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:776
msgid ""
"Set ``torch._inductor.config.max_autotune = True`` or "
"``TORCHINDUCTOR_MAX_AUTOTUNE=1`` to enable max-autotune for all supported"
" Triton kernels."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:780
msgid "Or, for more fine-grained control:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:782
msgid ""
"``torch._inductor.config.max_autotune_gemm = True`` or "
"``TORCHINDUCTOR_MAX_AUTOTUNE_GEMM=1``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:784
msgid "To enable tuning for ``gemm`` kernels only."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:786
msgid ""
"``torch._inductor.config.max_autotune_pointwise = True`` or "
"``TORCHINDUCTOR_MAX_AUTOTUNE_POINTWISE=1``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:788
msgid ""
"To enable tuning for ``pointwise``, ``reduction``, ``persistent "
"reduction``, and ``foreach`` kernels."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:791
msgid ""
"``torch._inductor.config.max_autotune_gemm_backends`` or "
"``TORCHINDUCTOR_MAX_AUTOTUNE_GEMM_BACKENDS``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:792
msgid ""
"Selects the candidate backends for ``mm`` auto-tuning. Defaults to "
"``TRITON,ATEN``. Limiting this to ``TRITON`` might improve performance by"
" enabling more fused ``mm`` kernels instead of going to :doc:`rocBLAS "
"<rocblas:index>` and/or :doc:`hipBLASLt <hipblaslt:index>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:798
msgid ""
"To expand the autotuning search space, set "
"``TORCHINDUCTOR_MAX_AUTOTUNE_GEMM_SEARCH_SPACE=\"EXHAUSTIVE\"``. For flex"
" attention autotuning, use "
"``TORCHINDUCTOR_MAX_AUTOTUNE_FLEX_SEARCH_SPACE=\"EXHAUSTIVE\"``. Note "
"that exhaustive autotuning is very slow and can significantly increase "
"compile time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:805
msgid ""
"Inference can see large improvements on AMD GPUs by utilizing "
"``torch._inductor.config.freezing=True`` or the "
"``TORCHINDUCTOR_FREEZING=1`` variable, which in-lines weights as "
"constants and enables constant folding optimizations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:809
msgid ""
"Enabling ``inductor``’s cpp_wrapper might improve overhead. This "
"generates C++ code which launches Triton binaries directly with "
"``hipModuleLaunchKernel`` and relies on `hipification`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:813
msgid ""
"``torch._inductor.config.cpp_wrapper=True`` or "
"``TORCHINDUCTOR_CPP_WRAPPER=1``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:815
msgid ""
"Convolution workloads might see a performance benefit by specifying "
"``torch._inductor.config.layout_optimization=True`` or "
"``TORCHINDUCTOR_LAYOUT_OPTIMIZATION=1``. This can help performance by "
"enforcing ``channel_last`` memory format on the convolution in "
"TorchInductor, avoiding any unnecessary transpose operations. Note that "
"``PYTORCH_MIOPEN_SUGGEST_NHWC=1`` is recommended if using this."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:821
msgid ""
"To extract the Triton kernels generated by ``inductor``, set the "
"environment variable ``TORCH_COMPILE_DEBUG=1``, which will create a "
"``torch_compile_debug/`` directory in the current path. The wrapper codes"
" generated by ``inductor`` are in one or more ``output_code.py`` files "
"corresponding to the FX graphs associated with the model. The Triton "
"kernels are defined in these generated codes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:829
msgid "Composable Kernel backend"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:831
msgid ""
"You can enable the Composable Kernel (``CK``) backend by appending ``CK``"
" to the comma-separated list of backends. This allows the auto-tuning "
"process to use kernels from the Composable Kernel library."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:834
msgid ""
"``torch._inductor.config.max_autotune_gemm_backends`` or "
"``TORCHINDUCTOR_MAX_AUTOTUNE_GEMM_BACKENDS``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:836
msgid ""
"Install the Composable Kernel library's Python wrapper via pip using the "
"following command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:842
msgid ""
"This wrapper library is responsible for constructing a list of kernel "
"instances available in the Composable Kernel library, as well as storing "
"the kernel instance C++ includes in a known location (so clang can look "
"into these paths when compiling the ``gemm`` auto-tune candidates)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:845
msgid ""
"``matmul`` (with ``float16`` and ``bfloat16`` inputs, row-major X, row-"
"major or column-major W)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:846
msgid ""
"``addmm`` (with ``float16`` or ``bfloat16`` X, W and Bias; row-major X, "
"row-major or column-major W; Bias can be broadcast either along row-major"
