#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1403
msgid "Use all eight GPUs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1405
msgid ""
"In an :ref:`MI300X architecture <mi300x-node-level-arch-fig>`, there are "
"dedicated links between each pair of GPUs in a fully connected topology. "
"Both MI300X and MI350X share this 8-GPU fully connected design. "
"Therefore, for collective operations, the best performance is achieved "
"when all 8 GPUs and, hence, all the links between them are used. In the "
"case of 2- or 4-GPU collective operations (generally less than 8 GPUs), "
"you can only use a fraction of the potential bandwidth on the node."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1415
#, python-format
msgid ""
"MI350X uses 2 IODs (vs 4 on MI300X) with a faster direct connection and "
"Infinity Fabric links at 38.4 Gbps (vs 32 Gbps), yielding 1,075.2 GB/s "
"P2P ring aggregate bandwidth (vs 896 GB/s) — roughly 20% higher."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1420
msgid ""
"The following figure shows an :doc:`MI300X node-level architecture "
"</conceptual/gpu-arch/mi300>` of a system with AMD EPYC processors in a "
"dual-socket configuration and eight AMD Instinct MI300X GPUs. The MI300X "
"OAMs attach to the host system via PCIe Gen 5 x16 links (yellow lines). "
"The GPUs use seven high-bandwidth, low-latency AMD Infinity Fabric™ links"
" (red lines) to form a fully connected 8-GPU system."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1432
msgid ""
"MI300 Series node-level architecture showing 8 fully interconnected "
"MI300X OAM modules connected to (optional) PCIe switches via re-timers "
"and HGX connectors. MI350 Series systems use the same 8-GPU fully "
"connected topology."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1439
#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/system-setup/prerequisite-system-validation.rst:15
#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/training/benchmark-docker/previous-versions/megatron-lm-v24.12-dev.rst:93
#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/training/benchmark-docker/previous-versions/megatron-lm-v25.3.rst:102
#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/training/benchmark-docker/previous-versions/pytorch-training-v25.3.rst:71
msgid "Disable NUMA auto-balancing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1441
msgid ""
"In order to reduce performance variability and also achieve better "
"performance, you need to make sure that NUMA auto-balancing is disabled "
"on the node."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1445
msgid ""
"Check whether NUMA auto-balancing is disabled, by running the following "
"command: ``cat /proc/sys/kernel/numa_balancing`` and checking whether the"
" output is ``0``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1449
msgid ""
"If the output is ``1``, you can disable NUMA auto-balancing by running "
"the following command: ``sudo sysctl kernel.numa_balancing=0``. For more "
"details, see `AMD Instinct MI300X system optimization "
"<https://instinct.docs.amd.com/projects/amdgpu-docs/en/latest/system-"
"optimization/mi300x.html#disable-numa-auto-balancing>`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1457
msgid "Disable ACS for multi-node RCCL"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1459
msgid ""
"Check if ACS is disabled with ``sudo lspci -vvv | grep -i \"acsctl\"``. "
"This will print many lines. Check if there are any that show "
"``SrcValid+``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1462
msgid ""
"If there are any ``SrcValid+``, then use the following ``disable_acs.sh``"
" script to disable ACS (requires ``sudo``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1515
msgid "Run RCCL-Unittests"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1517
msgid ""
"In order to verify RCCL installation and test whether all parts and units"
" of RCCL work as expected you can run the RCCL-Unittests which is "
"explained in `<https://github.com/ROCm/rccl?tab=readme-ov-file#tests>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1524
msgid "NPKit profiler"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1526
msgid ""
"To collect fine-grained trace events in RCCL components, especially in "
"giant collective GPU kernels you can use the NPKit profiler explained in "
"`<https://github.com/ROCm/rccl?tab=readme-ov-file#npkit>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1533
msgid "RCCL-tests"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1535
msgid ""
"RCCL-tests are performance and error-checking tests for RCCL maintained "
"in `<https://github.com/ROCm/rccl-tests>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1538
msgid ""
"These tests are one of the best ways to check the performance of "
"different collectives provided by RCCL. You can select collectives, "
"message sizes, datatypes, operations, number of iterations, etc., for "
"your test, and then rccl-tests deliver performance metrics such as "
"latency, algorithm bandwidth, and bus bandwidth for each case."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1547
msgid "Use one-process-per-GPU mode"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1549
msgid ""
"RCCL delivers the best performance for collectives when it is configured "
"in a one-process-per-GPU mode. This is due to the fact that for a one-"
"process-per-multiple-GPUs configuration, you can run into kernel launch "
"latency issues. This is because ROCm serializes kernel launches on "
"multiple GPUs from one process which hurts performance."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1558
msgid "RCCL in E2E workloads"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1560
msgid ""
"Use the following environment variable to increase the number of channels"
" used by RCCL when using RCCL in end-to-end workloads to potentially "
"improve the performance:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1571
msgid "Triton kernel performance optimization"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1573
msgid ""
"Triton kernel optimization encompasses a variety of strategies aimed at "
"maximizing the efficiency and performance of GPU computations. These "
"strategies include :ref:`optimizing overall GPU resource utilization "
"<mi300x-triton-gpu-utilization>`, :ref:`tuning kernel configurations "
"<mi300x-autotunable-kernel-config>`, and :ref:`leveraging specific "
"hardware features <mi300x-assembly-analysis>` to achieve higher "
"throughput and lower latency."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1584
msgid "Auto-tunable kernel configurations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1586
msgid ""
"Auto-tunable kernel configuration involves adjusting memory access and "
"computational resources assigned to each compute unit. It encompasses the"
" usage of :ref:`LDS <mi300x-cu-fig>`, register, and task scheduling on a "
"compute unit."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1590
msgid ""
"The GPU contains global memory, local data share (LDS), and registers. "
"Global memory has high access latency, but is large. LDS access has much "
"lower latency, but is smaller. It is a fast on-CU software-managed memory"
" that can be used to efficiently share data between all work items in a "
"block. Register access is the fastest yet smallest among the three."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1600
msgid ""
"Schematic representation of a CU in CDNA2 / CDNA3 / CDNA4 architectures. "
"Each CU has 4 SIMDs, which are grouped into 2 SIMD pairs (SP). Each SP "
"has a 128 bytes/clock bus to LDS. But 2 SP cannot access LDS at the same "
"time. So the actual read bandwidth of LDS is 128 bytes/clock. MI300X also"
" has direct L1->LDS. MI350's direct L1->LDS has larger vectorization."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1606
msgid ""
"The following is a list of kernel arguments used for tuning performance "
"and resource allocation on AMD GPUs, which helps in optimizing the "
"efficiency and throughput of various computational kernels."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1610
msgid "``num_stages=n``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1611
msgid ""
"Adjusts the number of pipeline stages for different types of kernels. On "
"AMD GPUs, set ``num_stages`` according to the following rules:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1614
msgid "For kernels with a single GEMM, set to ``2``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1616
msgid ""
"For kernels with two GEMMs fused (Flash Attention, or any other kernel "
"that fuses 2 GEMMs), set to ``1``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1619
msgid ""
"For kernels that fuse a single GEMM with another non-GEMM operator (for "
"example ReLU activation), set to ``2``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1622
msgid "For kernels that have no GEMMs, set to ``1``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1626
msgid ""
"MI350X's 160 KB LDS and doubled read bandwidth may allow higher "
"``num_stages`` (3 or 4) for single-GEMM kernels. Benchmark to verify."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1629
msgid "``waves_per_eu=n``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1630
msgid ""
"Helps to manage Vector General Purpose Registers (VGPR) usage to achieve "
"desired occupancy levels. This argument hints to the compiler to reduce "
"VGPR to achieve ``n`` occupancy where ``n`` is a number. The goal is to "
"achieve a certain occupancy level for each Execution Unit (EU, also "
"called :ref:`SIMD Unit <mi300x-cu-fig>`) to achieve better latency or "
"throughput. For more information on how to compute occupancy, see :ref"
":`mi300x-compute-kernel-occ`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1638
msgid "This argument is useful if:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1640
msgid "The occupancy of the kernel is limited by VGPR usage, and"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1642
msgid ""
"The current VGPR usage is only a few above a boundary in :ref:`Occupancy "
"related to VGPR usage in an Instinct MI300X GPU <mi300x-occupancy-vgpr-"
"table>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1647
msgid "Occupancy related to VGPR usage in an Instinct MI300X GPU."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1651
msgid "Occupancy related to VGPRs usage on an Instinct MI300X GPU"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1653
msgid ""
"For example, according to the table, each Execution Unit (EU) has 512 "
"available VGPRs, which are allocated in blocks of 16. If the current VGPR"
" usage is 170, it will be rounded up to 176 due to the allocation "
"granularity. In this case, the occupancy is limited to 2 waves per EU "
"because :math:`176 \\times 3 > 512`. So, if you set ``waves_per_eu`` to "
"3, the LLVM backend will attempt to reduce VGPR usage so that it might "
"fit 3 waves per EU."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1660
msgid "``BLOCK_M``, ``BLOCK_N``, ``BLOCK_K``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1661
msgid ""
"Tile sizes to be tuned to balance the memory-to-computation ratio. The "
"goal is to minimize the memory transfer from global to shared and reuse "
"memory across different threads. This needs to be tuned. The tile sizes "
"should be large enough to maximize the efficiency of the memory-to-"
"computation ratio but small enough to parallelize the greatest number of "
"workgroups at the grid level."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1670
msgid ""
"MI350X's 160 KB LDS (vs 64 KB on MI300X) enables larger tile sizes. "
"Explore larger ``BLOCK_M/N/K`` values to leverage the increased LDS and "
"doubled read bandwidth."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1674
msgid "``matrix_instr_nonkdim``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1675
msgid ""
"Experimental feature for Flash Attention-like kernels that determines the"
" size of the Matrix Fused Multiply-Add (MFMA) instruction used."
msgstr ""

