#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1729
msgid "MLIR analysis"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1731
msgid ""
"Triton includes the following layouts: **blocked**, **shared**, "
"**sliced**, and **MFMA**."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1733
msgid ""
"Use the Triton GPU Intermediate Representation (IR) to identify the "
"memory in which each computation takes place."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1736
msgid "Use the environment variable ``MLIR_ENABLE_DUMP`` to dump MLIR:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1742
msgid ""
"The following is a snippet of IR from the Flash Attention decode ``int4``"
" KV program. It is to de-quantize the ``int4`` key-value from the "
"``int4`` data type to ``fp16``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1785
msgid ""
"From the IR snippet, you can see ``i32`` data is loaded from global "
"memory to registers (``%190``). With a few element-wise operations in "
"registers, it is stored in shared memory (``%269``) for the transpose "
"operation (``%270``), which needs data movement across different threads."
" With the transpose done, it is loaded from LDS to register again "
"(``%276``), and with a few more element-wise operations, it is stored to "
"LDS again (``%298``). The last step loads from LDS to registers and "
"converts to the dot-operand layout (``%300``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1794
msgid ""
"The IR snippet uses the LDS twice. The first is for the transpose, and "
"the second is to convert a blocked layout to a dot operand layout. "
"There’s an opportunity to optimize performance by using LDS once."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1801
msgid "ISA assembly analysis"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1803
msgid ""
"To generate ISA, ``export AMDGCN_ENABLE_DUMP=1`` when running the Triton "
"program. The generated ISA will be printed as standard output. You can "
"dump it to a file for analysis."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1807
msgid ""
"Ensure ``global_load_dwordx4`` is used in the ISA, especially when the "
"global memory load happens in the loop."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1810
msgid ""
"In most cases, the LDS load and store should use ``_b128`` to minimize "
"the number of LDS access instructions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1813
msgid ""
"The AMD ISA has ``s_waitcnt`` instruction to synchronize the dependency "
"of memory access and computations. The ``s_waitcnt`` instructions can "
"typically have two signals in the Triton context:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1817
msgid ""
"``lgkmcnt(n)``: ``lgkm`` stands for LDS, GDS (Global Data Share), "
"Constant, and Message. It is often related to LDS access. The ``n`` "
"indicates the number of data accesses can still be ongoing before moving "
"on to the next step. For example, if ``n`` is ``0``, wait for all "
"``lgkm`` access to finish before continuing. If ``n`` is ``1``, move on "
"even if ``1`` ``lgkm`` access is still running asynchronously."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1825
msgid ""
"``vmcnt(n)``: ``vm`` represents vector memory. This happens when vector "
"memory is accessed, for example, when global load moves from global "
"memory to vector memory. The variable ``n`` is the same as the previous "
"setting."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1830
msgid "Generally recommended guidelines are as follows."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1832
msgid "Vectorize memory access as much as possible."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1834
msgid "Ensure synchronization is done efficiently."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1836
msgid ""
"Overlap of instructions to hide latency, but it requires thoughtful "
"analysis of the algorithms."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1839
msgid ""
"If you find inefficiencies, you can trace it back to LLVM IR, TTGIR and "
"even TTIR to see where the problem comes from. If you find it during "
"compiler optimization, activate the MLIR dump (``export "
"MLIR_ENABLE_DUMP=1``) and check which optimization pass caused the "
"problem."
msgstr ""

#: ../../how-to/rocm-for-ai/inference-optimization/workload.rst:1848
